<https://w3id.org/plato#identity_object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The second SpatialEntity in an identity assertion. Often a record in\n        another authority, such as a GeoNames feature or a Wikidata item,\n        which this range makes a SpatialEntity. That is intended: in PLATO\n        an authority record is a SpatialEntity like any other, identified by\n        its own URI, with its origin recorded in plato:namespace.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#certainty> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Epistemic certainty, from 0.0 (completely uncertain) to 1.0\n        (completely certain). On an Attestation it is the contributor's\n        confidence in the bundle as a whole. On a facet node (Name, Geometry,\n        Timespan, Type, PropertyValue) it is the confidence in that value\n        alone, and may differ from the attestation's overall certainty.\n        Certainty reflects the limits of evidence: better evidence could in\n        principle raise it. Distinct from fuzziness (the referent has no\n        sharp boundary) and from spatial_precision (how finely a location\n        is specified). No rdfs:domain, so it can qualify any node.\n\n        It is never the source's own confidence: how firmly a source asserts\n        what it says (hedging, hearsay, doubt) is plato:source_stance.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#identity_certainty> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Confidence in the identity assertion, from 0.0 to 1.0. This is\n        the epistemic certainty of the asserter, distinct from identity_type\n        which describes the semantic nature of the relationship.\n        A closeMatch can have high certainty ('I'm confident these are\n        close but not exact') and an exactMatch can have low certainty\n        ('I think they're identical but I'm not sure').\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#certainty_level> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Certainty as a word rather than a number: a CertaintyLevel, such as\n        plato:Certain, plato:LessCertain or plato:Uncertain (the three of Linked\n        Places Format), or one a project declares. Use it when certainty is\n        stated in words, by a dataset or on a project's own scale, rather than\n        inventing a number for plato:certainty; the two may also be given\n        together. It is the same confidence as plato:certainty, the\n        contributor's or editor's, not the source's: a source that hedges or\n        reports is recorded with plato:source_stance. Like\n        plato:certainty it has no rdfs:domain, so it can qualify an Attestation\n        or any facet node.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Challenges> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Contradicts> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#source_crs> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Original coordinate reference system. Typically 'EPSG:4326' (WGS84),\n        but may reference historical or custom CRS identifiers for historical\n        maps. Enables proper interpretation of coordinate values.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#value_literal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The value, where it is a literal: a number, a string, a date. Use\n        plato:value_json instead where the value is structured.\n    "@en .
