<https://w3id.org/plato#sequence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The position of a member in an ordered whole, as one source gives it:\n        a station on a route, a stop on an itinerary, a segment of a road.\n        It sits on the attestation about the member, which relates_to the\n        route, itinerary or network with plato:MemberOf; the relates_to target\n        is what the number orders, so one place can hold different positions\n        on different routes. Lower numbers come first.\n\n        Two members with the same number are alternatives at that point (two\n        branches of a road); a source that disagrees with another about the\n        order is recorded as its own attestations, with a meta-attestation\n        where a judgement is made between them. A member with no sequence is\n        in an order the source does not give. The members of an itinerary\n        carry their own timespans, from arrival to departure, and the order of\n        a journey known only from its dates may be left to those timespans.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#match_parameters> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The settings that produced the Candidate's score, as text (JSON\n        suggested): a threshold, the weights given to name, place and type,\n        the reference sources searched. With plato:algorithm_version it lets\n        a score be reproduced or explained. Optional; its form is the\n        software's own, since it describes a run of that software rather\n        than making a claim about places.\n    "@en .
