_:na519a30634a746a18aecd69d7dc797e5b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Geometry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#Geometry> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Gazetteer> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A mutable, versioned workspace of SpatialEntities and Attestations,\n        owned by a contributor or team. Replaces the WHG v3\n        concepts of Dataset, Dataset Collection, and Place Collection.\n        A Gazetteer can be incrementally edited, enriched, and corrected\n        without re-ingestion.\n\n        A Gazetteer's attestations need not be about SpatialEntities it\n        defines. It may attest evidence about SpatialEntities defined in other\n        Gazetteers, identified by their IRIs, without redefining them: a\n        correction to another project's place, a link from a record to an\n        existing place, a curator's selection of places for a collection.\n        Such an attestation adds evidence. It does not change the entity's\n        identity or make this Gazetteer its owner, and its source, contributor\n        and status are this Gazetteer's. The append-only rule of a published\n        Gazetteer applies to the attestations it contains, not to everything\n        said elsewhere about its entities. In PLATO JSON such attestations go\n        in an attestation-centric document, whose 'about' gives the entity's\n        IRI; a place-centric document declares every SpatialEntity it lists\n        (plato:contains_entity), so it lists only its own.\n\n        As a dcat:Dataset, a Gazetteer is titled with dcterms:title and\n        licensed with dcterms:license. Not with plato:authority_title or\n        plato:licence: their domains are plato:Authority and plato:Dataset,\n        so using them would make the Gazetteer one of the Authorities it\n        contains attestations about.\n\n        Described for discovery and citation (the FAIR principles), again in\n        the vocabularies a catalogue already reads: dcterms:creator for the\n        people to cite (plato:gazetteer_owner, its owner, is not necessarily\n        an author); dcat:keyword; dcterms:spatial for the area it covers, as\n        an address; dcterms:temporal for the years it covers, a\n        dcterms:PeriodOfTime with dcat:startDate and dcat:endDate;\n        dcat:landingPage; and void:uriSpace, the base from which its entities'\n        IRIs are made. A published Gazetteer (plato:gazetteer_status\n        'published') must state its dcterms:license: data others are to reuse\n        needs its terms stated. All the other descriptive properties are\n        optional.\n\n        Versions, and so citations that reproduce. A SpatialEntity's evidence\n        grows and is corrected while its identity stays the same, so a\n        citation of it must say which state was meant. For the same reason\n        as above, a Gazetteer's version uses DCAT 3's own properties, not a\n        PLATO one: dcat:version for its version indicator, and, for a frozen\n        snapshot published under its own IRI, dcat:isVersionOf (the living\n        Gazetteer) and dcat:previousVersion (the snapshot before). A\n        SpatialEntity is then cited as its IRI plus the Gazetteer version,\n        one number per gazetteer rather than one per entity, as PeriodO cites.\n\n        Once a Gazetteer's plato:gazetteer_status is 'published', its\n        attestations are append-only, and this is normative: an attestation is\n        never deleted or changed. A correction is a new attestation with a\n        meta-attestation (plato:Supersedes, plato:Contradicts), and a\n        withdrawal is a meta-attestation of type plato:Retracts. Each carries\n        plato:created. The state as of any moment D is then a filter over the\n        data: the attestations created by D, less those superseded or\n        retracted by D. A supersession or retraction takes effect only while\n        it holds itself, so retracting a retraction restores its target, and\n        a chain of them resolves the same way; a cycle is an error in the\n        data. A platform can offer that state on request, by\n        datetime negotiation on the entity's IRI (RFC 7089, Memento); PLATO\n        recommends this but does not require it, since it is behaviour of a\n        server, not of data.\n    "@en .
