<https://w3id.org/plato#name_type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        What the name denotes. The list is open: 'toponym' (place name),\n        'chrononym' (period or era name), 'ethnonym' (people or ethnic\n        group), 'demonym' (inhabitants), 'odonym' (street or road name) and\n        'hydronym' (water body) are the starter values, and a project may\n        add others ('oronym', 'hagionym') provided it documents them in its\n        gazetteer metadata. Multiple types are possible (a name that\n        functions as both toponym and ethnonym). plato.schema.json carries\n        the same open list; earlier releases closed it there, and the two\n        serialisations now agree.\n\n        This classifies the referent, not the status of the form. Whether a\n        string is an attested spelling, an editorial headword or a derived\n        search form is a property of the attestation (plato:form_status),\n        because the same string can have a different status in different\n        attestations.\n    "@en .
