<https://w3id.org/plato#form_status> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The status of the name form this attestation contributes: read in the\n        cited source, chosen by the cited authority, or derived by the\n        contributing project. Should point to a concept in a SKOS concept\n        scheme. The starter concepts are:\n\n        - plato:Attested: a form read in the cited source. The default, and\n          usually omitted.\n        - plato:Headword: the editorial or preferred form under which the\n          cited authority files the entry ('Bunsty Hundred'). A scholarly\n          decision rather than a reading, and typically the form users search\n          for. Another authority may choose differently, which is why it is a\n          sourced attestation and not a label on the SpatialEntity.\n        - plato:Normalised: a form derived from an attested one for matching\n          or search ('Bunstow(e)' resolved to 'Bunstowe'), attested by no\n          source and carrying no independent evidential value. It may point\n          to the attestation it was derived from through a meta-attestation.\n        - plato:Reconstructed: a form inferred but not attested, the\n          philologists' asterisked form.\n        - plato:Preferred: the form the contributing project prefers for\n          display and search ('Ranny (Fort)'). A project decision, so the cited\n          source may show a different form.\n\n        A different axis from plato:name_type, which classifies what the name\n        denotes: a headword is still a toponym. It sits on the Attestation,\n        not on the Name, because the same string can be a reading in one\n        attestation and a headword in another. The WHG ingestion prototype\n        reached the same placement independently: its preferred-name flag is\n        on the attestation, not on the toponym record.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#relation_domain> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Valid subject type for this relation."@en .
