<https://w3id.org/plato#occurrence_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The capacity in which the attested form occurs in the source. Should\n        point to a concept in a SKOS concept scheme. The starter concepts are:\n\n        - plato:Direct: the form refers to the SpatialEntity directly. The\n          default, and usually omitted.\n        - plato:InPersonalName: the form occurs only as an element of a\n          personal name, a surname or by-name such as 'Willelmus de\n          Bonestou'. This is the '(p)' of the place-name surveys, and it is\n          weaker evidence for the name's currency, and for the entity's\n          existence, than a direct attestation. Nothing else in the record\n          distinguishes Bonestou the hundred from de Bonestou the man.\n        - plato:InFieldName: the form survives only as an element of a later\n          minor name, such as a field name.\n\n        The editorially resolved 'ibidem' of the surveys is not an occurrence\n        context. It is a statement about the citation, and belongs on the\n        Citation as plato:attribution_status.\n\n        Not a certainty. A '(p)' attestation may be entirely certain as an\n        attestation; what differs is the kind of evidence it constitutes, and\n        folding that into a lower plato:certainty would discard the reason.\n    "@en .
