<https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A bundle of evidence asserting that a particular SpatialEntity had a particular\n        Name, Geometry, Type, or combination thereof, during a particular\n        Timespan, according to a particular Authority. The Attestation node\n        carries only metadata; its semantic content is defined entirely by its\n        outgoing relationships.\n\n        IMPORTANT: Attestations are document nodes in the graph, not edges.\n        They are instances of plato:Attestation (a class), not instances of\n        owl:ObjectProperty. This design enables meta-attestations and full\n        provenance tracking.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#authority_uri> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#ConnectedTo> <https://w3id.org/plato#inverse_label> "connected_to" .
<https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_url> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#gazetteer_status> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "status"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#WorkplaceOf> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The target person or group worked, or held office, at the subject, for the attested timespan: Linked Traces' heldOffice, and work generally."@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#property_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#value_json> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Contributor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A person, team, or automated process that creates attestations.\n        Aligned with both foaf:Agent and prov:Agent for provenance\n        chain-of-custody tracking.\n\n        Contributors should be identified using persistent identifiers where\n        possible. For persons, ORCID (https://orcid.org/) is strongly\n        recommended and may be required by specific implementations. ORCID\n        identifiers should use the full URI format (https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000)\n        rather than just the numeric portion, as ORCID is particular about\n        proper URI formatting for linked data interoperability.\n\n        For organizations, ROR (https://ror.org/) or ISNI identifiers are\n        recommended. Proper identification ensures accurate attribution,\n        enables citation, and supports research assessment.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#type_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#attribution_status> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "attribution status"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#identity_type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#sourced_by> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#type_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#publisher> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#transliteration_system> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "transliteration system"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#form_status> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#publisher> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
_:na519a30634a746a18aecd69d7dc797e5b16 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:na519a30634a746a18aecd69d7dc797e5b17 .
_:na519a30634a746a18aecd69d7dc797e5b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_orcid> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        ORCID identifier for person contributors. Can be provided in two formats:\n\n        1. Compact format: 0000-0000-0000-0000 (sixteen digits in groups of four)\n        2. URI format: https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000 (preferred)\n\n        The URI format (https://orcid.org/...) is preferred and required for\n        proper linked data representation. ORCID is particular about always\n        including the https:// protocol and domain prefix.\n\n        While optional in the general ontology, specific implementations\n        (e.g., WHG) may require ORCID for person contributors to ensure\n        proper attribution and citation.\n\n        ORCID provides persistent identification for researchers, enabling\n        proper credit and reducing name ambiguity.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#edtf_string> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#authority_title> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "title"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#edtf_string> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#AttributionStated> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "attribution stated"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#candidate_source> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#geo_wkt> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subPropertyOf> <http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#asWKT> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#BirthplaceOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#relation_label> "birthplace_of" .
<https://w3id.org/plato#inverse_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Inverse label (e.g. 'has_capital')."@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#declares_relation_type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#SpatialEntity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        An entity whose identity is bound up with space, whether its spatial\n        extent is attested directly, applies notionally (as with the region to\n        which a period applies), or is derived from its constituents (as with a\n        route or a network, whose extent is that of its members). A\n        SpatialEntity need not have any attested geometry: lost, imagined and\n        unlocated entities are admissible.\n\n        A curated selection of other places, such as a thematic collection, is\n        not a SpatialEntity: it is a Gazetteer, whose attestations are about\n        the places it selects.\n\n        What kind of entity it is (settlement, route, region, administrative\n        unit and so on) is not fixed by the class. It is established by\n        Type attestations, so a classification is a claim rather than an axiom.\n\n        SpatialEntities are not defined by any single attestation. They are the\n        point of convergence for many attestations from different sources,\n        periods and contributors.\n\n        PLATO defines no Place class and gives \"place\" no prescribed\n        meaning. Many SpatialEntities are what people would call places and\n        some are not, and the word is used differently across languages and\n        disciplines. The ontology therefore neither equates a SpatialEntity\n        with a place nor defines a place in terms of SpatialEntities or\n        their attestations. Where the word appears in this document it is\n        used in its ordinary sense.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#locator> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Where in the cited work the evidence sits: 'p. 412', 'f. 12v', 'Table\n        VI, col. 3, row 88', 's.v. Athlone', 'no. xix'. An uninterpreted\n        string, deliberately: sources locate themselves inconsistently, and\n        normalising the value would destroy exactly the information it is\n        there to carry. The same position and shape as PeriodO's source\n        locator.\n\n        A property of this citation, not of the source: one Source for a\n        printed gazetteer, a different locator on each attestation drawn\n        from it. Not to be confused with the spatial sense of 'locator' in\n        the plato:geometry_role comment, a proxy point standing in for an\n        entity.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#StanceTentative> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://w3id.org/plato#SourceStanceScheme> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#locator> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/plato#Citation> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Bundles> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Beyond> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "beyond"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#TranscriptionAccurate> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#type_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Human-readable label for this type."@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#computed> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        True when this value, or this whole attestation, was worked out by\n        software from other data rather than taken from a source: an\n        itinerary's timespan from the timespans of its stops, a route's\n        geometry from its stations. A computed value is not evidence. A\n        consumer may show it, but must not take it in as an attestation of\n        its own: it should compute the value again from the members, or leave\n        it out and say so. Absent or false, a value is attested.\n\n        It is for a value derived from other statements in the same data,\n        which any consumer holding them could derive again. A figure a\n        project works out from its own source material and publishes as a\n        finding (letters counted from a register, a median taken over them)\n        is not computed in this sense: it is attested, citing the work that\n        produced it as a Source or Dataset derived_from the material it was\n        worked out from.\n\n        It is not plato:form_status Normalised (a name form an editor\n        derived from an attested one), nor a Candidate (a match an algorithm\n        suggests). Like the qualification properties it has no rdfs:domain,\n        so it can mark an Attestation or any facet node.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#match_parameters> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "match parameters"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#occurrence_count> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The number of times the attested form occurs in the cited source or\n        passage: the '(3 X)' of a place-name survey. A count of occurrences\n        of the form in this source, not of independent sources. It belongs to\n        the attestation, not to the Name, which may occur elsewhere.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#generated_at> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "generated at"@en .
