<https://w3id.org/plato#occurrence_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "occurrence context"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Name> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A toponym or appellation for a SpatialEntity. Names are independent, reusable\n        entities. The association between a Name and a SpatialEntity is always mediated\n        by an Attestation, which provides temporal scope and provenance.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#relative_distance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Distance from the anchor, in metres. Primarily used for\n        relative geometries. May carry its own certainty or fuzziness.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#identity_basis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#attests_type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Links an Attestation to a Type classification it assigns to the SpatialEntity.\n        An attestation may assert multiple types or no type at all.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#transliteration_system> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The transliteration/romanization system used (e.g., 'Pinyin' for\n        Chinese, 'BGN/PCGN' for Arabic, 'ISO 259' for Hebrew, 'Hepburn' for\n        Japanese). Enables proper interpretation and comparison of romanized\n        forms.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Supports> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Provides additional evidence for the target attestation."@en .
