<https://w3id.org/plato#IdentityRelation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        An assertion that two SpatialEntities refer to the same real-world entity.\n        Carries its own provenance (who asserted it, on what basis),\n        certainty (how confident the assertion is), and identity semantics\n        (exactMatch, closeMatch, related).\n\n        Identity relations are distinct from scholarly entity-to-entity\n        relations, which describe substantive historical relationships\n        between different entities. They are also distinct from Candidates,\n        which are algorithm-generated candidates not yet confirmed by\n        human review.\n\n        An IdentityRelation is one attributed claim among many, never a\n        canonical statement of fact. A platform must not materialise identity\n        relations into merged records or minted identifiers, nor let one\n        outweigh contrary evidence by fiat: it is weighed like any other\n        evidence, and someone else may say the opposite (an attestation with\n        plato:negated bundling an exactMatch says two entities are not the\n        same).\n\n        Identity relations asserted together, as when a person accepts a\n        cluster of matches, are bundled by one Attestation\n        (plato:attests_identity), which carries their shared provenance and\n        is withdrawn or amended as a unit. A consumer may take the transitive\n        closure of exactMatch relations within one such attestation, and must\n        not chain identity relations across different attestations, nor\n        chain closeMatch, related or unspecified ones at all: A the same as B\n        and B the same as C, asserted by different people, is not anyone's\n        assertion that A is C.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#identity_asserted_at> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#identity_object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "identity object"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#value_datatype> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
_:na519a30634a746a18aecd69d7dc797e5b18 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <https://w3id.org/plato#Period> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#start_precision> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "start precision"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#language> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        BCP 47 language tag for this name (e.g., 'ar' for Arabic, 'zh-Hans'\n        for Simplified Chinese). Recommended: use ISO 639-3 three-letter codes\n        within BCP 47 for better granularity (e.g., 'ara', 'cmn').\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#citation_function> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Why the source is cited: as the evidence for the attestation, as a\n        source of its data, as related reading, and so on. The value is a\n        property of CiTO, the Citation Typing Ontology\n        (http://purl.org/spar/cito/): cito:citesAsEvidence,\n        cito:citesAsDataSource, cito:citesAsRelated, cito:citesForInformation,\n        cito:citesAsRecommendedReading, or another of the properties below\n        cito:cites. Only CiTO properties are used, so that every consumer reads\n        the same function the same way; a project whose own vocabulary has\n        other terms maps them to CiTO when it organises its data.\n\n        Without it, 'this source is the evidence' and 'see this source for\n        more' are the same link, which is the distinction an attestation model\n        exists to make: 143,418 of Pleiades' 244,759 references are 'see\n        further', and only 21,652 are cited as evidence. Absent, the function\n        is unstated, as before; a consumer should not assume evidence.\n\n        Only a property CiTO defines is a valid value. An IRI in the CiTO\n        namespace is not enough: Pleiades writes cito:seeFurther and\n        cito:seeAlso, which CiTO does not define, so the JSON Schema and the\n        tables list the accepted properties exactly.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#promoted_from> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Links an IdentityRelation to the Candidate from which it was\n        promoted, preserving the algorithmic provenance. Optional:\n        identity relations can also be created directly by human\n        assertion without a prior Candidate.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#RelationType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Bundles> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Groups the target with other related attestations."@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#contains_identity_relation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Links a Gazetteer to an IdentityRelation asserted within it. The\n        submission profiles carry identity relations at the top level of a\n        document, beside the entities and attestations, and this is the\n        property that top level maps to.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#created> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Timestamp when this attestation was created. This is the contribution\n        date, not the historical timespan being attested (use attests_timespan\n        for that). In a published Gazetteer it is the time axis along which an\n        earlier state is recomputed, so every attestation and meta-attestation\n        there should carry it.\n\n        Semantically equivalent to PROV-O's prov:generatedAtTime. We don't use\n        formal rdfs:subPropertyOf to avoid importing PROV-O constraints.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#related_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
