<https://w3id.org/plato#attests_property> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Links an Attestation to a PropertyValue it asserts of the SpatialEntity — a\n        population, a valuation, an elevation, a market's trading day. An\n        attestation may assert several properties or none.\n\n        These are among the commonest things a source says about an entity, and\n        they carry exactly the provenance and temporal scoping the attestation\n        pattern exists to handle: a population figure is a claim, by a named\n        source, about one SpatialEntity, over a Timespan, with a certainty. The\n        attestation's own timespans scope the property as they scope every\n        other facet in the bundle.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#language> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Authority> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointUnionOf> _:na519a30634a746a18aecd69d7dc797e5b1 .
<https://w3id.org/plato#gazetteer_status> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#identity_type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "identity type"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#attests_type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#certainty_level> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#modified> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Timestamp when this attestation was last modified, while its\n        Gazetteer is still a draft. Once the Gazetteer is published its\n        attestations are append-only (see plato:Gazetteer): a published\n        attestation is not modified, and a change is a new attestation with a\n        meta-attestation, so that the state at any earlier moment can be\n        recomputed and every citation of it reproduces.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#AlternativeTo> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#LessCertain> <https://w3id.org/plato#authority_title> "less certain"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#source_stance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        How firmly the source itself asserts what this attestation bundles: a\n        concept in plato:SourceStanceScheme, plato:StanceAsserted (the default,\n        usually omitted), plato:StanceReported, plato:StanceTentative or\n        plato:StanceDoubted, or one a project declares. It records the source,\n        not whoever encodes it: plato:certainty and plato:certainty_level are\n        the contributor's or editor's confidence, and an editor may be quite\n        certain that a source hedged. Nor is it plato:attribution_status, which\n        says whether the source is named where the evidence occurs.\n\n        Doubt is not denial. A source that raises a thing and declines to\n        settle it is plato:StanceDoubted; one that says it is not so is\n        plato:negated. A source that passes on a claim and names whom it\n        reports can have that work cited as well.\n\n        It qualifies the attestation as a whole, as plato:negated does, so\n        bundle one facet per attestation where the source's stance differs\n        between facets. A consumer that cannot express a stance reads the\n        attestation as asserted, which overstates the source but does not\n        invert it. So, unlike plato:negated, no consumer is required to leave\n        it out, but a converter should report the loss.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Period> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Period"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#relates_to> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#geo_json> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#JSON> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#scheme_version> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty> .
<https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3060-0181> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/account> <https://github.com/docuracy> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#generated_at> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#authority_uri> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/plato#Authority> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#has_meta_type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "meta-attestation type"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#negated> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        True when the source states that what this attestation bundles is not\n        so: that a place had no market, that a name was not used, that a grant\n        was refused. The attestation is still about a real SpatialEntity (the\n        town that had no market), with the facet the source denies (a Type\n        'market'); nothing that never existed need be invented. A denial is a\n        statement a source makes, often a certain one, so it is neither low\n        certainty nor a meta-attestation, which needs a claim to contradict.\n        The 1886 return of market rights answered for every borough and\n        district in England and Wales, and 1,000 of its 1,813 rows are denials.\n\n        A consumer that does not implement this property will read a denial\n        as an assertion, the opposite of what the source says. So it must be\n        honoured: a tool that cannot express a denial in its target format\n        must leave the attestation out and report it, never write it as an\n        assertion. Bundle one denied facet per attestation, so that what is\n        denied is never in doubt. Absent or false, the attestation asserts.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#ContainedIn> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The subject SpatialEntity is contained in the target SpatialEntity, for\n        the attested timespan: a parish in a hundred, a hundred in a county.\n        Not an identity relation and not fixed by the class model; it is a\n        claim, with a source and a certainty, like any other attestation.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#start_earliest> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Literal> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#precision_km> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Uncertainty radius in kilometers. For point geometries, indicates\n        how far the true location might be from the stated coordinates.\n        For polygons, indicates boundary uncertainty. Can be multiple values\n        for heterogeneous geometries.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#WorkplaceOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#relation_domain> "SpatialEntity" .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Dataset> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A collection-level authority: a published gazetteer, a contributed\n        dataset, or an external authority corpus (GeoNames, Wikidata, TGN,\n        OSM, Pleiades). Carries metadata such as title, version, publisher,\n        and URI, and a licence (dcterms:license, as for any Authority).\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#source_crs> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#ResidenceOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#relation_range> "person or group" .
<https://w3id.org/plato#value_datatype> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "value datatype"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_url> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Retracts> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Citation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#SettingOf> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#end_earliest> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The earliest possible end date for this timespan. Together with\n        end_latest, defines the uncertainty range for when the interval ended.\n        May be null for unknown or deliberately open-end intervals.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#MemberOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#relation_domain> "SpatialEntity" .
<https://w3id.org/plato#source_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The original label as given in the contributing source, before\n        vocabulary mapping. For a structured PropertyValue this preserves the\n        unparsed original — 'Monday after Whitsun, 3 days' — which is what a\n        future reader will want to check against the document. On a Name it is\n        the form as printed where the toponym has been normalised; on a\n        Geometry, the coordinates as printed ('03-47S/13038E', '??'); on a\n        Timespan, the date as written ('about 1841'). On an Attestation it is\n        the source's own wording of the relation the attestation asserts\n        ('part of Berkshire', 'within Buckingham'), before that relation is\n        mapped to a RelationType; an attestation asserts at most one relation,\n        so the wording is never ambiguous. Like the qualification properties it\n        has no rdfs:domain, so it applies to any facet node or attestation.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Annotates> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Adds a remark about the target attestation, with its own source, without supporting, contradicting or refining it. The remark is the meta-attestation's notes."@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Beyond> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Timespan"@en .
