<https://w3id.org/plato#geometry_role> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        What this geometry depicts, as distinct from how precisely it is known\n        (plato:spatial_precision) or where its indexing point falls\n        (plato:repr_point). A SpatialEntity may be attested with several geometries at\n        once — a built extent, the location of a specific central feature, a\n        proxy standing in for the whole, the anchor of a map label — and without\n        a declared role a consumer cannot tell them apart, nor state which one\n        an analysis used.\n\n        Should point to a concept in a SKOS concept scheme (e.g. plato:Extent,\n        plato:FeaturePoint, plato:RepresentativePoint, plato:LabelAnchor,\n        plato:Itinerary). Using URIs rather than free text lets gazetteers that\n        record geometry differently be compared.\n\n        Note that role is orthogonal to precision: a proxy locator may be\n        surveyed to the metre and still not depict the SpatialEntity it stands for. A\n        parish church's coordinates contributed as a locator for a town are a\n        representative point of high precision, not an imprecise feature point.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#has_relation_type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#edtf_string> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/plato#Timespan> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#FeaturePoint> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The location of a specific central feature of the SpatialEntity, such as a market place or a church."@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#relative_bearing> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_affiliation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "affiliation"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Normalised> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A form derived from an attested one for matching or search, attested by no source and carrying no independent evidential value."@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#scheme_version> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#script> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/plato#Name> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#has_meta_type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The nature of a meta-attestation's relationship to its target attestation.\n        Should reference a controlled vocabulary concept defining the relationship\n        type. The starter concepts, declared in this ontology's MetaTypeScheme:\n\n        - plato:Contradicts: asserts the target attestation is incorrect\n        - plato:Supports: provides additional evidence for the target attestation\n        - plato:Supersedes: replaces the target attestation with newer information\n        - plato:Refines: provides more precise information than the target\n        - plato:Challenges: questions the target attestation's validity\n        - plato:Bundles: groups the target with other related attestations\n        - plato:DerivedFrom: this attestation's value was derived from the\n          target's (a normalised search form from an attested spelling)\n        - plato:Annotates: adds a remark about the target, with its own source,\n          without supporting, contradicting or refining it; the remark is the\n          meta-attestation's notes\n        - plato:AlternativeTo: this attestation and the target are alternative\n          readings of the same evidence, at most one of them right\n        - plato:Retracts: the target attestation is withdrawn by whoever made\n          it, and kept only so that earlier states can be reproduced\n\n        Using SKOS concepts rather than strings enables vocabulary management\n        and multilingual labels.\n    "@en .
_:na519a30634a746a18aecd69d7dc797e5b19 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:na519a30634a746a18aecd69d7dc797e5b20 .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Itinerary> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#RelativeQualifierScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "relative qualifier"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#MetaTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Starter concepts for plato:has_meta_type. The nature of a meta-attestation's relationship to its target attestation."@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato> <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#keyword> "place"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#identity_sourced_by> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#relates_to> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Links an Attestation to the second entity of a relationship between\n        the Attestation's subject entity (via attests_about) and this target.\n        The semantic nature of the relationship is specified by\n        plato:has_relation_type, which references a RelationType authority.\n\n        Example: An attestation about Constantinople that relates_to the\n        Byzantine Empire with relation_type \"capital_of\" asserts that\n        \"Constantinople was the capital of the Byzantine Empire\".\n\n        The target is usually another SpatialEntity in the same dataset. It\n        may instead be an entity that is not spatial and is described\n        elsewhere: a person, an object or an event, identified by its IRI\n        (an attestation about Lumbini that relates_to the Buddha, with\n        plato:BirthplaceOf). There is deliberately no rdfs:range, so such a\n        target is not inferred to be a SpatialEntity. Give an external target\n        a plato:related_label. Platforms should show relations to entities\n        that are not spatial apart from spatial ones, and leave them out of\n        containment, clustering and matching.\n    "@en .
