<https://w3id.org/plato#start_earliest> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The earliest possible start date for this timespan. Together with\n        start_latest, defines the uncertainty range for when the interval began.\n        Use ISO 8601 format (e.g., '1453-05-29') or a year of at least four\n        digits ('0921', '-12000') for year-level precision; deep-time years\n        have as many digits as they need, as in xsd:gYear. May be null for unknown or deliberately\n        open-beginning intervals.\n\n        The range is rdfs:Literal rather than a single datatype, because one\n        property carries values of three shapes. A plain string is conformant;\n        a producer that can type by shape should emit xsd:gYear for a year,\n        xsd:date for a day and xsd:dateTime otherwise, so that a store can\n        index and compare the bounds. The same applies to start_latest,\n        end_earliest and end_latest.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#ConnectedTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The subject and target are directly connected, in no particular\n        direction: an attestation that A is connected to B says the same as\n        one that B is connected to A. For a connection that runs one way, use\n        plato:LeadsTo. A connection known in both directions from sources that\n        each give one is better recorded as two LeadsTo attestations, each\n        with its own source, than as one ConnectedTo.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#type_identifier> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "type identifier"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#StanceTentative> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "tentative"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#attests_geometry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/plato#Attestation> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#contributor_identifier> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#DeathplaceOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#inverse_label> "died_at" .
<https://w3id.org/plato#certainty_level> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "certainty level"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#related_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A name for the target of plato:relates_to, for display: 'the Buddha',\n        'denarius of Hadrian, BM 1872,0709.470'. It belongs on the attestation\n        rather than on the target, because the target may be someone else's\n        IRI, about which this dataset makes no other statement, and a platform\n        showing a place cannot reliably look up every IRI it links to. Give it\n        whenever the target is not a SpatialEntity in the same dataset; it is\n        harmless, but redundant, when the target is. It names the target; the\n        source's own wording of the relation is plato:source_label.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#timespan_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#property_type> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#WorkplaceOf> <https://w3id.org/plato#authority_title> "workplace of"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#attests_geometry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Links an Attestation to a Geometry it asserts for the SpatialEntity. An\n        attestation may assert multiple geometries (e.g., point and polygon)\n        or no geometry at all (e.g., name-only attestations from textual sources).\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Certain> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/plato#CertaintyLevel> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#UpstreamOf> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Upstream of the anchor along a watercourse."@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#timespan_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
