<https://w3id.org/plato#periodo_uri> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Link to a PeriodO period definition (https://perio.do/). When a timespan\n        corresponds to a standardized period in PeriodO, this property enables\n        alignment and allows PLATO to inherit PeriodO's temporal bounds, spatial\n        coverage, and cultural context. Example: 'https://n2t.net/ark:/99152/p06v8w4rb38'\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#HasEnd> <https://w3id.org/plato#relation_domain> "SpatialEntity (a segment)" .
<https://w3id.org/plato#ConnectedTo> <https://w3id.org/plato#authority_title> "connected to"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#type_identifier> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        The concept's IRI in a controlled vocabulary: an AAT, Wikidata or\n        GeoNames concept, an OpenStreetMap tag. It should be a full IRI, not a\n        bare code ('PPL', 'Q687188', 'stream') whose vocabulary is known only\n        from somewhere else: that is what lets types from different sources be\n        compared. An OpenStreetMap tag's IRI carries its key\n        (https://wiki.openstreetmap.org/wiki/Tag:waterway=stream), since the\n        value alone is ambiguous. A source's own category that no vocabulary\n        holds is recorded as the Type's label and plato:source_label, with no\n        identifier, rather than as a code made up for it. The IRI is written\n        as a literal typed xsd:anyURI, never as an IRI object: that is what\n        the JSON-LD context and the spreadsheet tables produce, and one form\n        lets queries compare identifiers without converting them.\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#Source> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#geo_wkt> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "geometry WKT"@en .
<https://w3id.org/plato#DuringReignOf> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/plato> .
<https://w3id.org/plato#EndsAt> <https://w3id.org/plato#relation_domain> "SpatialEntity (a segment)" .
<https://w3id.org/plato#timespan_label> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        Human-readable label for this timespan (e.g., 'Byzantine period',\n        'Early Bronze Age', 'Ming Dynasty'). Particularly useful for named\n        periods referenced across multiple attestations. The date exactly as a\n        source writes it ('c. 925', 'a. 1300') is plato:source_label.\n    "@en .
