<https://w3id.org/plato#PropertyValue> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\n        A quantitative or descriptive attribute of a SpatialEntity, expressed as a\n        property drawn from an external vocabulary together with its value.\n        Covers the many things a historical source says that are neither a\n        name, a location, a period nor a classification — populations,\n        valuations, elevations, recurrence rules for markets and fairs.\n\n        The property itself is identified by URI rather than enumerated here,\n        so the vocabulary stays open and delegated (Wikidata properties,\n        CIDOC-CRM, project vocabularies), in the same way that Type delegates\n        its classifications to AAT.\n\n        Values may be literals (with a datatype and optional unit) or\n        structured objects whose shape is declared by plato:value_type. As a\n        facet node, a PropertyValue may carry the qualification properties\n        (certainty, fuzziness) like any other.\n\n        Where the value is another SpatialEntity, use an entity-to-entity\n        relation instead; this class is for attributes of the entity. A person\n        or organisation connected to it (the grantee of a market right, an\n        owner, a lord of the manor) is such an attribute: the value is the\n        agent's IRI, ideally in an authority such as Wikidata or VIAF, with\n        value_datatype xsd:anyURI, and the property names the role, for\n        example http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P127 ('owned by'). This\n        asserts nothing false, and needs no agent class: PLATO's own agent\n        classes (Contributor, Organization) describe who recorded the data.\n\n        A figure from a statistical table (a census count, a toll receipt) is\n        a PropertyValue that is also a qb:Observation of the W3C RDF Data\n        Cube vocabulary. It carries qb:dataSet (the table, a qb:DataSet), one\n        direct statement for each of its coordinates with the table's\n        qb:DimensionProperty predicates, one for each fact about the figure\n        with qb:AttributeProperty predicates (sdmx-attribute:obsStatus for a\n        cell's state), and plato:universe for the figure it is a part or share\n        of. So the county is not said to have 'class 1': the observation is.\n        The measure stays plato:property_type and plato:value_literal, and the\n        area and date stay on the attestation; a cube export adds the direct\n        measure statement, sdmx-dimension:refArea and refPeriod. A printed dash\n        is an observation with an obsStatus and no value, never 0. Money is\n        an exact integer in the smallest unit of its system of account\n        (farthings, for sterling amounts with halfpennies), the unit an IRI\n        from a fitting authority or minted by the encoding project, ideally\n        naming a unit of account in a named system ('penny sterling'); PLATO\n        mints no currency and converts nothing. A coordinate the source does\n        not give is an explicit 'unknown' code. A table mixing measures is one\n        qb:DataSet per measure. Dimension values are concepts, with the\n        source's label as skos:prefLabel, in code lists the encoding project\n        publishes where none exists. A statistical table in spreadsheet form\n        keeps its own CSV on the Web description and links to places by\n        place_id. The design, and its testing on three corpora, is issue #14.\n\n        A latitude given without a longitude (a source's 'at 9.5 degrees\n        north') is a PropertyValue, not a Geometry, since it is not a location:\n        property http://www.w3.org/2003/01/geo/wgs84_pos#lat, the value in\n        decimal degrees. A longitude alone is the same, with wgs84_pos#long.\n    "@en .
